instruction_following_eval (IFEval)
===================================

This directory is a partial copy of the IFEval evaluator released by Google
Research with

> Jeffrey Zhou, Tianjian Lu, Swaroop Mishra, Siddhartha Brahma, Sujoy Basu,
> Yi Luan, Denny Zhou, Le Hou. *Instruction-Following Evaluation for Large
> Language Models.* arXiv:2311.07911, 2023.

* Upstream: <https://github.com/google-research/google-research/tree/master/instruction_following_eval>
* Copyright The Google Research Authors.
* Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0; see `LICENSE` in this
  directory for the licence text.

CAST uses it for the instruction-following (IFEval) utility measurement in
`run_utility.py`; nothing in this repository changes how a response is scored.

Files
-----

    __init__.py
    evaluation_lib.py
    instructions.py
    instructions_registry.py
    instructions_util.py

The IFEval prompt set (`data/input_data.jsonl` upstream) is not redistributed
here; see the README section "Obtaining data and artifacts".

Modifications
-------------

In accordance with section 4(b) of the Apache License, the changes made to the
original files are:

1. `evaluation_lib.py`, `instructions.py`, `instructions_registry.py`: the
   sibling imports `from instruction_following_eval import <module>` were made
   relative (`from . import <module>`), because this package is vendored one
   level down, under `third_party/`, and is imported as
   `third_party.instruction_following_eval`. Each changed file carries a comment
   at the import saying so. No evaluation logic was touched.
2. `__init__.py` (empty) is not part of the upstream directory; it is here so the
   vendored copy is a regular package.

Everything else is upstream verbatim. The copy predates the type-checker
pragma comments (`# pyrefly: ignore[...]`) that upstream has since added to
`evaluation_lib.py` and `instructions.py`; stripping those comments from today's
upstream files and making the one import relative reproduces all three changed
files exactly, byte for byte. `instructions_util.py` is unchanged from upstream
and therefore carries no notice.

The per-file headers read "Copyright 2026 The Google Research Authors", which is
what upstream's own headers currently read — the google-research monorepo
rewrites the year across the tree each year (the same files read 2023 when
IFEval was first published in November 2023, and 2024 in January 2024). The
headers are therefore left exactly as upstream publishes them.
